messages:
alex: Quick note  we're getting a steady trickle of onboarding tickets about import styles for the JS SDK. Users are confused about require vs import, default vs named exports, and TS typing failures.
sophie: Can you summarize the most common user setups hitting errors?
alex: Sure: 1) Node projects using CommonJS (no \"type\":\"module\") that `require()` the package and get undefined or a namespace object, 2) Node projects using ESM (\"type\":\"module\") where `import { InferenceClient }` errors with \"named export not found\", 3) TypeScript projects without esModuleInterop that can't use default imports, 4) Edge runtimes (Vercel/Cloudflare/Deno) which are ESM-only and need explicit examples. Also a few Bun oddities.
indra: Typical support threads show two error messages: \"Named export 'InferenceClient' not found from '@redwood/inference'\" and \"TypeError: Cannot read property 'InferenceClient' of undefined\" after require().
build-eng: Sounds like our published package layout doesn't match the quickstart assumptions. We should avoid changing exports mid-sprint but we can land docs + examples fast. Long-term fix is to normalize build outputs so package ships both CJS and ESM with a clear exports map.
sophie: Proposal: add a tiny \"Import patterns\" section to the SDK README and create four minimal examples: examples/js/cjs, examples/js/esm, examples/js/ts, examples/js/edge. Copy/paste that works reduces tickets.
alex: Draft snippets to include (short and copy/paste):
```
// Node CommonJS (package.json WITHOUT \"type\": \"module\")
const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
// guard the common shapes: sometimes the client is on Redwood.InferenceClient or Redwood.default.InferenceClient
const Client = Redwood.InferenceClient || Redwood.default?.InferenceClient;
const client = new Client({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

// Node ESM default import (\"type\": \"module\")
import Redwood from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new Redwood.InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

// ESM named import (preferred for edge runtimes)
import { InferenceClient } from '@redwood/inference';
const client2 = new InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

// TypeScript fallback (if you can't enable esModuleInterop)
import Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
const clientTs = new (Redwood as any).InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
```

indra: Also add a short tsconfig snippet to the docs so TS users have an easy path: `{"compilerOptions": {"esModuleInterop": true, "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true}}`. That fixes most of the TS pain.
sophie: For edge runtimes call out explicitly: \"Edge runtimes (Vercel Edge, Cloudflare Workers, Deno Deploy) are ESM-only; use the named/ESM import snippet above.\" Include a tiny Vercel Edge example: 
```
import { InferenceClient } from '@redwood/inference';
export default async function handler(req) {
  const c = new InferenceClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
  const out = await c.generate({ prompt: 'hi' });
  return new Response(JSON.stringify(out));
}
```

alex: For more advanced users, include a brief package.json exports example to explain conditional exports and why bundlers pick certain entries: 
```json
{
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./dist/esm/index.mjs",
      "require": "./dist/cjs/index.cjs"
    }
  },
  "main": "./dist/cjs/index.cjs",
  "module": "./dist/esm/index.mjs",
  "types": "./dist/index.d.ts"
}
```

build-eng: Operational follow-ups: INF-420 opened to normalize publish outputs (emit dist/cjs + dist/esm + matching .d.ts) and add an exports map during publish. We'll also add CI smoke tests that run the example apps in node-cjs, node-esm, tsc compile, and a lightweight vercel dev run for edge examples.
indra: Support triage tip: include quick local commands that users/support can run to inspect how their runtime resolved the package: `node -e "console.log(require.resolve('@redwood/inference'))"` and `node -e "console.log(require('@redwood/inference'))"`. For ESM shells: `node -p "(await import('@redwood/inference'))"`. Add these to the troubleshooting section.
support-bot: I'll add a playbook entry linking to the new README cheatsheet and the inspection commands so support can triage faster.
docs-bot: Draft docs PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/630 - adds \"JS SDK: Import patterns (CJS / ESM / TS / Edge)\" with links to the four example apps. Please review.
sophie: One more small item: add a single-line note in the quickstart landing page offering the right snippet depending on detected environment (if we can't detect, show both CJS and ESM). That will cut the number of users who blindly paste the wrong one.
alex: Agreed. For now docs + examples + support playbook will reduce friction; we'll follow up with the publish normalization patch tracked in INF-420. Thanks all  closing this thread once PR merges.
